# 0531-k60-audio-amp-probe-call.patch is deliberately NOT listed: the kernel link
# cannot resolve its call site. The call is `extern int pong_audio_amp_probe(void)
# __attribute__((weak));` guarded by `!= NULL`, and clang does emit N_WEAK_REF
# (verified in bsd_init.o's symbol table) -- but this ld64-port rejects an undefined
# weak function reference in the static/dynamic images the kernel is linked as, and
# accepts it only in the -kext form the kernel does not use. The result was
# `Undefined symbols for architecture arm64e: "_pong_audio_amp_probe", referenced
# from: _bsd_init in bsd_init.o; l_pong_audio_amp_probe$auth_ptr$ia$42271 in
# bsd_init.o` (snapshot-build.log:1173089, round 15). Stage 0 has no audio: the file
# stays here so the audio lane can list it again once the object that defines the
# probe is really in the link. The test is not "the conf/files hook is fixed" --
# 406198f5 fixed the hook's placement, yet scripts/k60-conf-files-hook.py is still
# absent, so PongAudioAmp.o is still never compiled and the symbol still has no
# definition. List this patch again only when
# `grep -c 'PongAudioAmp.o' <tree>/build/xnu/snapshot-obj/RELEASE_ARM64_BCM2837/link.filelist`
# is at least 1. A definition that returns 0 would not do either: `AUDIO27 OK` has
# to tell "not wired" from "wired to an empty shell".
0532-k60-cpu-start-completion.patch
# arm64_isa_compatibility.h emits the q-register spellings (sha256su0 q0, q1)
# unless __apple_build_version__ > 5010000, i.e. unless Apple's assembler is
# the one reading the file. This tree is built by upstream clang, whose
# integrated assembler rejects those operands: the fileset reference build
# stops with "invalid operand for instruction" on sha256su0/sha256h and never
# writes link.filelist. The v-register form is accepted by both assemblers, so
# every clang gets V and only non-clang toolchains keep q. Same change as the
# iOS 16 series carries in zzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzz-k60-v-crypto-syntax.patch;
# 12377 has no equivalent, so it is re-issued here as a numbered entry.
0533-k60-v-crypto-syntax.patch

# The iOS 27 machine configuration is BCM2837 and does not define PONG, so
# drivers/ArmCpu/ArmCpu.cpp's four extern "C" PSCI entry points had no
# definition at all in 12377: the fileset reference link stopped with
# Undefined symbols _psci_smp_init, _psci_smp_start_cpu, _psci_smp_cpu_running
# and _psci_smp_cpu_off (snapshot-build.log:1171748). This is the real
# implementation from the 16 series reshaped for 12377, not a stub: a stub that
# always returned failure would weld "this tree cannot do SMP" into the kernel
# and its "PSCI start FAIL" line would later be read as a hardware fault.
